<h1><span lang="EN-GB">New Cylinder - Non-Interactive</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-US">This command is accessible from</span> <i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Datuming &gt; Non-Interactive &gt; Cylinder</b></span></i>.</p>
<p class="Description"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> <span class="Command">New Cylinder</span> <span lang="EN-US">command creates new datum cylinder</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US"><img src="../images/Cylinder.GIF" width="384px" height="338px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Name</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US">Name for the cylinder. The default name is Cylinder N, where N is the number of the subsequent newly created datum cylinder.</span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US">Select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list to define co-ordinates of the first point on the axis of the cylinder or type-in the co-ordinates manually.</span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US">Select an existing datum point, sphere, or circle from the list to define co-ordinates of the second point on the axis of the cylinder or type-in the co-ordinates manually.</span></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Radius</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription"><span lang="EN-US">Radius of the cylinder. It must be a positive non-zero value.</span></p>
<p class="whs5"><span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 1</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">and</span> <span lang="EN-US" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Point 2</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">must be two different points and should not have to coincide. In case of sphere and circle, their centres are used as points.</span></p>
